# Overview
#
# Reports a phrase that dates a claim to the present (`the parser
# currently supports tables`, `at the time of writing`). The docs
# outlast the version that they describe, so the reader cannot tell
# whether the claim still holds. The fix deletes the time phrase and
# states what the system does (`the parser supports tables`). The rule
# sits at Recommended, because a tutorial uses the same phrases to
# narrate the reader's own code.
#
# Expression
#
# `currently` counts only before a word that names support or a limit
# (`currently supports`, `currently only`), after a helping verb such as
# `does`, `cannot`, or `will`, or after `not`.
#
# `for now` skips a comma and a word that starts a tutorial's own next
# step, such as `let's` or `all`. By default, a Weir match stops at a
# comma, so `let scope "Sentence"` lets the `for now` guard cross one.
# The filter narrows the finding back to the phrase.
#
# A clause is a subject and its verb, such as `the tests pass`. `at the
# moment` counts only at the end of a clause. Its guard `moment!( )`
# takes any token but whitespace right after the phrase, so it covers
# punctuation and the end of a list item.
#
# `recently` counts only before a verb of change from the `chg` list
# (`recently added`), and not after `most` or `least`. `this year` skips
# `since` and `of` before it. Weir has no lookbehind, so each filter
# consumes the word before `recently` or `this year` and narrows the
# finding back to the phrase. Each filter needs a word before the
# phrase, so a sentence that opens with `Recently added` or `This year`
# passes.
#
# The other branches are exact phrases, such as `right now` and `at the
# time of writing`. `this year`, `last year`, and `this quarter` date a
# claim to the calendar. `still experimental`, `still in beta`, `still
# under development`, and `relatively new` date a claim to a release
# stage that ends.
#
# False Positives
#
# Bare `currently` is too common as runtime state (`the currently
# selected row`, `whether the item is currently available`), so it stays
# out. `TODO` stays out, because a code comment uses it as a marker.
#
# `For now,` before a tutorial's own next step defers a topic and dates
# nothing (`For now, let's focus on the parser`, `For now, all you need
# is the flag`). `we` still counts there, because a code comment writes
# `For now, we panic` for a limit of the code.
#
# Before a clause, `at the moment` means `when` (`spends its tokens at
# the moment it is called`). After `most` or `least`, `recently` orders
# events at run time (`the least recently added entry`). After `since`
# or `of`, `this year` names a time in a computation (`seconds since
# this year`).
#
# Related Rules
#
# ProseChangeNarration reports a change described from the past, and
# ProseRoadmap reports a claim promised for later. This rule reports a
# claim dated to the present.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 159 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 13 of 20 sampled
# hits dated a claim. Most misfires were tutorial narration about the
# reader's own code (`For now, the trait has no methods`).
expr chg [added, introduced, changed, removed, deprecated, released]
expr main [(currently [supports, support, only, unsupported, not, no, limited, lacks, lack, requires, require, returns, accepts, uses, defaults, ignores, handles, allows, disallows, broken, missing, unimplemented, experimental, deprecated]), ([does, doesn't, do, don't, cannot, can't, will, won't] currently), (not currently), (at the moment!( )), (right now), <(for now!(,( )[all, let's, let, just, you, you'll, ignore, focus, don't])), (for now)>, (at present), (as of now), (at this time), (as of today), (at the time of writing), (as of this writing), (in the current version), (in the current release), (in the latest version), (in the latest release), (in this version), (in this release), nowadays, (these days), lately, (until recently), <(![most, least] recently @chg), (recently @chg)>, <(![since, of] this year), (this year)>, (last year), (this quarter), (still [experimental, alpha, beta, unstable]), (still in [alpha, beta, preview]), (still under development), (still under active development), (relatively new)]
let message "Dated claim. Delete the time phrase and state what the system does."
let description "Flags phrases that date a claim to the current version."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Available reports whether the item is currently available."
allows "Cursor returns the currently selected row."
allows "The parser supports tables."
allows "TODO: handle the empty case."
allows "Reserve spends its tokens at the moment it is called."
allows "The cache evicts the least recently added entry."
allows "The table counts seconds since this year."
allows "For now, let's focus on the parser."
